          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Garden primarily signifies Edenic paradise: life, divine presence, fellowship with God, and creation lost and ultimately restored. Other meanings are contextual, such as Gethsemane, bridal intimacy, and spiritual fruitfulness.\",\"book_summary\":\"A garden signifies a planted collective of tree-symbolized nations or peoples arranged under a ruling gardener.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Garden = paradise or the life-giving presence of God, especially Eden lost and restored.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Garden = an organized collective of nations or peoples represented as trees under a gardener's rule.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book does not merely narrow the Eden/paradise motif to a particular kind of garden. It reclassifies the garden's symbolic occupants as corporate peoples and identifies the garden itself as their governed arrangement, replacing the consensus's central identification with paradise and divine fellowship.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"In the same symbolic sense, the garden cannot be identified primarily both as paradise/divine fellowship and as a collective of nations or peoples. The book's reading replaces the consensus referent rather than refining its boundaries.\",\"rationale\":\"None of the consensus responses or listed alternatives identifies a garden as an arrangement of tree-symbolized nations. Because the book presents this corporate-political referent as the term's core while explicitly distinguishing it from the commonly taught paradise view, the relationship is divergent rather than a refinement. Although the idea is also novel relative to the surveyed alternatives, its function here is to replace the recognized central identification.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Ezekiel 31:9 supports a garden containing multiple trees and depicts Assyria through arboreal imagery, but the excerpt alone does not explicitly identify every tree as a nation or people, nor establish that the garden itself symbolizes their governed collective. That conclusion depends on the broader chapter and the separate symbolic identification of trees.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
